WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:05.338
Passepartout headed quickly to the port to reserve three cabins on the Carnatic

2
00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:09.441
. He was delighted that Aouda was going to continue the journey with

3
00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:14.851
them. When he found Fix walking up and down the docks, he was not

4
00:00:14.852 --> 00:00:22.273
surprised. Fix seemed very worried. The detective had good reason to be upset—the warrant for Mr. Fogg's arrest had not arrived yet.

5
00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:25.457
It would not reach Hong Kong for two more days!

6
00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:32.423
Hong Kong was the last English territory on Mr. Fogg's route, and if Fix did not arrest him here, Fogg would escape.

7
00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:36.904
"Well, Monsieur Fix," said Passepartout as he caught up to the detective.

8
00:00:36.905 --> 00:00:40.090
"Have you decided to go with us as far as America?"

9
00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:44.112
"Yes," returned Fix through clenched teeth.

10
00:00:44.113 --> 00:00:52.498
"Good!" exclaimed Passepartout, laughing. "I knew you could not separate yourself from us. Come and purchase your ticket."

11
00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:57.384
Fix and Passepartout entered the steamer office and reserved four cabins.

12
00:00:57.385 --> 00:01:02.184
As the clerk handed them the tickets, he said that the repairs were complete on the Carnatic

13
00:01:02.185 --> 00:01:07.285
. The steamer would be ready to leave that evening, not the next morning as had been announced.

14
00:01:07.286 --> 00:01:11.023
"That will suit my master all the better," said Passepartout.

15
00:01:11.024 --> 00:01:13.255
"I will go and let him know."

16
00:01:13.256 --> 00:01:19.415
Fix decided to make a bold move. It was time to tell Passepartout the truth about his master.

17
00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:23.301
He invited Passepartout to go to a tavern on the quay.

18
00:01:23.302 --> 00:01:28.187
"I really should inform my master of the steamer's change in schedule," said Passepartout.

19
00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:33.580
"We'll just have a quick drink," Fix promised. "I want to tell you something."

20
00:01:33.581 --> 00:01:39.039
"Well . . . maybe just a quick one," Passepartout said with a smile.

21
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.760
On entering the tavern, they noticed a large cot at the end of the room.

22
00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:49.983
Several people were lying on cushions and some were in a deep sleep.

23
00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:54.855
Passepartout realized, with mild surprise, that they were in an opium house.

24
00:01:54.856 --> 00:01:57.917
They ordered a bottle of wine and filled their glasses.

25
00:01:57.918 --> 00:02:01.612
"We must have a serious talk," said Fix.

26
00:02:01.613 --> 00:02:06.038
"A serious talk!" said Passepartout, emptying his glass.

27
00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:08.904
"Let's do that tomorrow. I don't have time now."

28
00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:13.296
"What I have to say concerns your master," said Fix.

29
00:02:13.297 --> 00:02:17.317
Passepartout looked at Fix, whose face had an odd expression.

30
00:02:17.318 --> 00:02:24.014
Fix placed his hand on Passepartout's arm and, lowering his voice, said, "You have guessed who I am?"

31
00:02:24.015 --> 00:02:27.387
"Of course!" said Passepartout, smiling.

32
00:02:27.388 --> 00:02:31.212
"Then I am going to tell you everything," Fix began.

33
00:02:31.213 --> 00:02:34.483
"But I know everything already," said Passepartout.

34
00:02:34.484 --> 00:02:38.618
"Those gentlemen have put themselves to a useless expense."

35
00:02:38.619 --> 00:02:45.618
"Useless?" said Fix with surprise. "It's clear that you don't know how large the sum is."

36
00:02:45.619 --> 00:02:50.235
"Of course I do," said Passepartout. "Twenty-thousand pounds!"

37
00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:53.918
"Fifty-five thousand!" said Fix.

38
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:56.342
"What!" cried Passepartout.

39
00:02:56.343 --> 00:03:04.879
"Fifty-five thousand pounds," said Fix. "Now, if you help me, I'll share my reward with you."

40
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.517
"Help you?" cried Passepartout, his eyes wide open.

41
00:03:08.518 --> 00:03:15.211
"Yes, help me keep Mr. Fogg here for two or three days," said Fix.

42
00:03:15.212 --> 00:03:20.667
"It's a conspiracy then! And it is organized by the gentlemen of the Reform Club!"

43
00:03:20.668 --> 00:03:26.311
Passepartout was outraged. "Mr. Fix, my master is an honorable man.

44
00:03:26.312 --> 00:03:31.668
He is trying to win the wager fairly, and you are trying to postpone him. Shame on you!" The

45
00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:34.722
detective passed his hand across his forehead. He

46
00:03:34.723 --> 00:03:37.991
would have to break the news delicately. "Listen

47
00:03:37.992 --> 00:03:44.875
to me," said Fix. "I am a police detective, sent here by the London office. Mr.

48
00:03:44.876 --> 00:03:49.613
Fogg's wager is only a pretext for leaving the country. On

49
00:03:49.614 --> 00:03:59.296
September 28, a sum of fifty-five-thousand pounds was stolen from the Bank of England. Phileas Fogg is the bank robber." "Nonsense!"

50
00:03:59.297 --> 00:04:04.136
yelled Passepartout, slamming his fist on the table. "Would

51
00:04:04.137 --> 00:04:08.470
you like to be arrested as his partner?" asked Fix abruptly. "No!"

52
00:04:08.471 --> 00:04:10.927
said Passepartout. "I

53
00:04:10.928 --> 00:04:17.072
advise you to help me detain him, or you risk just such an arrest," said Fix. "I

54
00:04:17.073 --> 00:04:22.363
refuse!" said Passepartout. He held his head between his hands. He

55
00:04:22.364 --> 00:04:28.895
couldn't believe what he was hearing. He filled his glass again and drank. Fix

56
00:04:28.896 --> 00:04:31.834
put his arm around Passepartout's shoulder. "If

57
00:04:31.835 --> 00:04:38.719
that is how you feel," he said in a companionable way, "please forget that I have said anything." Some

58
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.552
pipes full of opium lay on the table, and Fix slipped one into Passepartout's hand. Passepartout

59
00:04:44.553 --> 00:04:52.878
drew several puffs, and he fell unconscious on the table. While

60
00:04:52.879 --> 00:05:00.027
Fix was delaying Passepartout at the opium house, Mr. Fogg was escorting Aouda around the shops of Hong Kong. A

61
00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:06.292
lady, as Mr. Fogg was well aware, could not be expected to travel with only a carpetbag. In

62
00:05:06.293 --> 00:05:09.837
the evening they returned to the hotel for an elegant dinner. At

63
00:05:09.838 --> 00:05:13.070
bedtime Passepartout still had not returned. If

64
00:05:13.071 --> 00:05:17.824
Phileas Fogg could have been astonished by anything, this might have done it. But,

65
00:05:17.825 --> 00:05:23.023
thinking that the Carnatic was not departing until the morning, he was not concerned. The

66
00:05:23.024 --> 00:05:30.510
next morning Mr. Fogg and Aouda prepared their bags and arrived at the quay to learn that the Carnatic had sailed the evening before. Upon

67
00:05:30.511 --> 00:05:39.860
hearing the news, Mr. Fogg's comment to Aouda was simply, "It is an accident, madam. Nothing more." Passepartout's

68
00:05:39.861 --> 00:05:43.297
Guidebook Well, there I was, asleep in an opium

69
00:05:43.298 --> 00:05:46.557
den! That Detective Fix, I hope I never see him

70
00:05:46.558 --> 00:05:50.426
again! Perhaps you had never heard of an opium den before reading about our adventures.

71
00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:56.102
Now you know that these dens are like bars where the drug opium is served and smoked through pipes.

72
00:05:56.103 --> 00:06:00.597
Beds are provided for people to lie down since opium makes people sleepy.

73
00:06:00.598 --> 00:06:04.068
Opium is a highly addictive drug made from poppies.

74
00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:11.437
It was used as a medicine in China starting in the fifteenth century but was banned in the eighteenth century due to its harmful effects.

75
00:06:11.438 --> 00:06:16.849
Despite the ban opium use in China flourished, and many Chinese people became addicted.

76
00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:22.337
That was because a powerful British company called the East India Company started growing opium in India

77
00:06:22.338 --> 00:06:25.743
and smuggling it into China in huge amounts!

78
00:06:25.744 --> 00:06:29.764
This business was so profitable that the British government let it continue.

79
00:06:29.765 --> 00:06:35.383
The Chinese went to war with the British twice to stop the smuggling, but the British won both times.

80
00:06:35.384 --> 00:06:39.524
As Chinese people emigrated around the world, they brought opium with them.

81
00:06:39.525 --> 00:06:48.231
Soon opium dens spread to large cities such as London and San Francisco.

